<article class="env-lemma" id="05UX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05UX">Lemma <span data-tag="05UX">7.32.9</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Let $p : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (pt) \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ be a point of the topos associated to $\mathcal{C}$. For any set $E$ there are canonical maps </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  E \longrightarrow (p_*E)_ p \longrightarrow E  \]
</div>
  <p> whose composition is $\text{id}_ E$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      There is always an adjunction map $(p_*E)_ p = p^{-1}p_*E \to E$. This map is an isomorphism when $E = \{ *\} $ because $p_*$ and $p^{-1}$ are both left exact, hence transform the final object into the final object. Hence given $e \in E$ we can consider the map $i_ e : \{ *\}  \to E$ which gives 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ p^{-1}p_*\{ *\}  \ar[rr]_{p^{-1}p_*i_ e} \ar[d]_{\cong } &  &  p^{-1}p_*E \ar[d] \\  \{ *\}  \ar[rr]^{i_ e} &  &  E }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       whence the map $E \to (p_*E)_ p = p^{-1}p_*E$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>